From 973003c6b92734836c44641750bd7598e01572e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:43:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/17] feat(mailbox): deliver remote mail directly to MX so
 [mailer] is optional

The integrated server could host mailboxes but still leaned on the [mailer]
transport for every remote recipient, so removing postfix only moved the
dependency. With OUTBOUND_MODE = direct, which is the new default, the server
resolves the recipient domain itself and delivers to its MX, and an instance
with only [mailbox] configured can send all of its mail.

Outbound path:
- group remote recipients by destination domain and queue them in a new
  mailbox_outbound table, so a slow or unreachable destination never blocks an
  SMTP session or a web request
- resolve MX in preference order, fall back to the domain's address record when
  no MX is published (RFC 5321 5.1), and refuse a null MX (RFC 7505)
- upgrade with STARTTLS when offered. Certificates are not verified by default:
  MX hosts routinely present names that do not match the record used to reach
  them, so demanding a valid chain would drop legitimate mail. This is the
  opportunistic model of RFC 7435; OUTBOUND_REQUIRE_TLS demands a verified
  chain instead
- classify 5xx as permanent and 4xx, DNS temporary failures and dropped
  connections as deferrals, retrying with a widening backoff until
  OUTBOUND_RETRY_MAX_AGE
- file a delivery status notice into the sender's mailbox when a message is
  finally given up on, standing in for the bounce an edge MTA would return

Queue rows are leased with a conditional update before a worker takes them, so
overlapping runs or a second instance on the same database cannot deliver the
same message twice.

Making [mailer] optional:
- add mailer.MailEnabled(), true when either transport can carry mail, and use
  it for the guards that previously required [mailer]: the mail queue and its
  notifier, password reset, registration confirmation, team invites and the
  admin send-email action all now work with [mailbox] alone
- the mailbox-aware sender routes remote recipients through the direct path and
  reports a clear error if something still reaches a nil [mailer] transport

OUTBOUND_MODE = relay keeps the previous behaviour for deployments behind a
smarthost or on hosts that cannot open outbound port 25.

Docs spell out that direct mode needs the operator to look like a legitimate
sender: static egress IP, forward-confirmed rDNS matching OUTBOUND_HELO, SPF
covering it and DKIM enabled.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Matheus Sampaio Queiroga <srherobrine20@gmail.com>
